    {
      "claim": "By November 1996 a prohibition against recruiting children under the age of fifteen into armed forces or groups had crystallized into customary international law.",
      "reasoning": "The text of Additional Protocol I (1977) obliges parties to refrain from recruiting children under fifteen (art. 77(2)). The Protocol was widely ratified and, even before universal ratification, its provisions were regarded as reflecting a norm of special protection for children that transcended treaty obligations. State practice of enacting national criminal statutes prohibiting child recruitment prior to 1996, together with the widespread acceptance of the Protocol’s rule, demonstrates the required elements of state practice and opinio juris, establishing the rule as customary.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "77(2)",
          "quoted_text": "The Parties to the conflict shall take all feasible measures in order that children who have not attained the age of fifteen years do not take a direct part in hostilities and, in particular, they shall refrain from recruiting them into their armed forces."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The customary prohibition against recruiting children under fifteen carries with it individual criminal responsibility for persons who enlist or use them in hostilities.",
      "reasoning": "Customary IHL imposes individual liability for breaches of its norms (as recognized by the jurisprudence of the ICTY, ICTR and the ICC). The Rome Statute (adopted 1998) codifies the recruitment of children under fifteen as a war crime (art. 8(2)(e)(vii) and art. 8(2)(b)(xxvi)), expressly stating that the conduct is punishable. The Statute’s inclusion of the element indicates that the drafters regarded the rule as already existing in customary law, and therefore the individual criminal responsibility attached to the norm predates the Statute. Consequently, by 1996 an individual who recruited children under fifteen could be held criminally liable under customary IHL.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(e)(vii)",
          "quoted_text": "Conscripting or enlisting children under the age of fifteen years into armed forces or groups or using them to participate actively in hostilities;"
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(xxvi)",
          "quoted_text": "Conscripting or enlisting children under the age of fifteen years into the national armed forces or using them to participate actively in hostilities."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The existence of national criminal legislation prohibiting child recruitment before 1996 demonstrates opinio juris and reinforces the customary status of the prohibition.",
      "reasoning": "Customary law requires both widespread state practice and a belief that such practice is legally required (opinio juris). The fact that a number of states had already criminalised the enlistment of children under fifteen in their domestic codes shows that states regarded the prohibition as a legal duty, not merely a policy choice. This domestic criminalisation, taken together with the treaty obligations of AP I, satisfies the two elements of customary law formation, confirming that the norm was binding on individuals by 1996.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Later treaty provisions, such as Article 4 of the Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child (2000), confirm the pre‑existing customary norm that armed groups must not recruit persons under eighteen, underscoring the continuity of the earlier fifteen‑year threshold as part of the same protective principle.",
      "reasoning": "Although the Optional Protocol was adopted after 1996, its language reflects the same protective principle that had already been articulated in AP I (1977) and had become customary. The Protocol’s explicit duty for armed groups not to recruit under eighteen, and its call on states to criminalise such conduct, demonstrates the international community’s acceptance of the underlying norm, reinforcing the argument that the norm existed customary before the Protocol’s entry into force.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "crc_opac",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "Armed groups that are distinct from the armed forces of a State should not, under any circumstances, recruit or use in hostilities persons under the age of 18 years."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

